Author:     totalwindupflightsystems <totalwindupflightsystems@gmail.com>
AuthorDate: Fri Sep 18 07:13:42 2026 -0500
Commit:     totalwindupflightsystems <totalwindupflightsystems@gmail.com>
CommitDate: Fri Sep 18 07:13:42 2026 -0500

    fix(cli): honor seed subcommand after server flags
    
    Addresses OB-GAP-071.
    
    `./off-by-one --db PATH seed` booted the HTTP server instead of seeding: the
    dispatch test was `os.Args[1] == "seed"`, and the stdlib flag package stops
    parsing at the first non-flag argument, so the server path consumed the argv,
    bound the port and served an empty catalog. The pre-fix HEAD binary serves
    GET /health -> 200 on that exact argv.
    
    Dispatch now finds the subcommand wherever the first positional argument sits:
    
    * cmd/off-by-one/dispatch.go (new) — seedDispatch(argv, server) walks argv with
      a probe FlagSet built from the LIVE server declarations (same names, same
      arity via the flag package's own IsBoolFlag test, values discarded), so
      `--db seed` still means "the database file named seed" and `--port 9000 seed`
      finds the subcommand. An explicit pre-seed -db is forwarded in its seed
      spelling (a -db after the subcommand still wins, since the seed FlagSet
      applies occurrences in order); any other pre-seed server flag is named on
      stderr as ignored rather than silently reinterpreted. --help/-h, unknown or
      malformed flags and an explicit --version stay on the server path, which
      prints the same usage/version output as before.
    * cmd/off-by-one/main.go — registerServerFlags() declares the server flag set;
      main() registers it before dispatch (declaring is side-effect free, no
      parsing or output), so the probe cannot drift from the real arity table as
      flags are added. The server path is otherwise untouched: .env loading,
      flag.Parse, help, version and every flag value behave exactly as before.
    * cmd/off-by-one/dispatch_test.go (new) — table tests for every recognised form
      (including the `--db seed` ambiguity and boolean flags), an arity invariant
      driven by the live server flag set, a seedRun round trip proving a forwarded
      -db lands the corpus in that database, and three real-binary subprocess
      tests: the acceptance argv (`--db PATH --port N seed`) must seed PATH from
      the cwd corpus, exit on its own and never bind the port; the historical
      `seed -db PATH` form; and server startup still parsing --port/--db, logging
      them and binding.
    
    Evidence: gofmt -l clean; `go build ./...`, `go vet ./...`,
    `go test ./... -short -p 1 -count=1` all pass. RED proof — reverting only the
    main.go wiring (helper files kept, package still compiles) fails
    TestSeedAfterLeadingServerFlagsSeedsAndNeverServes with "the invocation bound
    127.0.0.1:<port> — the HTTP server started instead of seeding (OB-GAP-071)" in
    0.79s; restoring the wiring turns it green. All tests use temp databases and a
    temp corpus; no live database, port or corpus file is touched.
    
    Co-authored-by: Alexis Okuwa <wojonstech@gmail.com>
